Alembic migrations for the Lightning Flight backend.

## Local dev / new environments

Migrations aren't wired into anything yet — `app.main` still calls
`Base.metadata.create_all()` on startup (see the comment above that
call in `backend/app/main.py`), so a fresh local DB or a fresh Render
deploy works exactly as it did before Alembic existed here.

## Generating a new migration

After changing a model in `backend/app/models/`:

    cd backend
    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "short description"

Then read the generated file in `alembic/versions/` before committing
it — autogenerate is a starting point, not something to trust blindly
(it won't catch a column rename, for instance; that shows up as a
drop + add unless you edit the migration to use `op.alter_column`).

## One-time production cutover (not done yet)

The existing Render database already has all three tables (created by
`create_all()`, not by Alembic), so Alembic doesn't know about them
yet. Before `Base.metadata.create_all()` can be removed from
`app/main.py`, this needs to happen once, in this order:

1. On the Render backend service (via its shell/console), run:

       cd backend
       alembic stamp head

   This tells Alembic "the database is already at this revision" —
   it does NOT run any migration or touch the schema, since the
   baseline migration (`alembic/versions/*_baseline.py`) matches what
   `create_all()` already built. Skipping this step and running
   `alembic upgrade head` instead would fail: it would try to
   `CREATE TABLE` on tables that already exist.

2. Update Render's start command for the backend service to run
   migrations before the app starts, e.g.:

       cd backend && alembic upgrade head && uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port $PORT

   (adjust to match whatever the current start command actually is —
   this repo doesn't commit a Procfile, so the exact command lives in
   the Render dashboard.)

3. Only after both of the above are confirmed working on a real
   deploy, remove the `Base.metadata.create_all()` call from
   `app/main.py` in a small follow-up PR. From that point on, every
   schema change ships as a new Alembic revision instead of a manual
   edit to the production database.
